## Section 5 — Stage 05: Advanced Memory Systems

Package: `src/langgraph_learning/stage05_advanced_memory_systems/`. Studio graph IDs:
`stage05_structured_memory`, `stage05_fact_collection`, `stage05_long_term_memory`,
`stage05_multi_memory`. Shared file: `configuration.py`.

Theme: the LangGraph **store** (cross-thread, user-scoped memory — distinct from the
checkpointer used in stage02/04, which is thread-scoped). All four lessons pair `InMemoryStore`
(long-term, keyed by `user_id`) with `MemorySaver` (short-term, keyed by `thread_id`)
simultaneously, via `builder.compile(store=store, checkpointer=checkpointer)`.

### 5.1 `configuration.py` — shared config dataclass

```python
@dataclass(kw_only=True)
class MemoryConfiguration:
    user_id: str = "default-user"

    @classmethod
    def from_runnable_config(cls, config=None) -> "MemoryConfiguration": ...
```

Resolves `user_id` from (in order) an environment variable named after the field in upper case
(`USER_ID`), then `config["configurable"]["user_id"]`, then the dataclass default. Used by
`agent_with_fact_collection.py`, `agent_with_long_term_memory.py`, and
`agent_with_multi_memory_coordination.py` (NOT `agent_with_structured_memory.py` — see §5.2).
Passed to `StateGraph(MessagesState, config_schema=MemoryConfiguration)` so LangGraph/Studio know
to expose `user_id` as a configurable field in the UI.

**Enhancement task:** this dataclass has exactly one field today. If any lesson needs a second
cross-cutting config value (e.g. a `memory_limit` or `namespace_prefix`), add it here rather than
inventing a parallel config mechanism.

### 5.2 `agent_with_structured_memory.py` — single structured user profile

- `UserProfile(BaseModel)`: `name`, `location`, `job` (all `Optional[str] = None`), `interests:
  List[str] = Field(default_factory=list)`.
- `_call_model_with_memory` reads `store.get(("profile", user_id), "user_profile")` — **one
  document per user, fixed key `"user_profile"`** — overwritten wholesale on every update (the
  merge happens because the extractor LLM is shown the existing profile as context and asked to
  "update" it — the merge logic lives in the prompt, not in code).
- `_update_profile_with_structured_output` uses a **separate** `extractor_llm` from the
  response-generating `llm` (`_assemble_profile_graph(llm, extractor_llm, ...)`) — allows using a
  cheaper/faster model for structured extraction than for conversational replies, a legitimate
  production cost-optimization pattern already baked in.
- **Verified inconsistency:** `_call_model_with_memory` and
  `_update_profile_with_structured_output` both do `config["configurable"]["user_id"]` directly
  (raw dict indexing), unlike the other three stage05 lessons which go through
  `MemoryConfiguration.from_runnable_config(config)`. This lesson raises a raw `KeyError` (not a
  clean validation error) if a caller forgets to supply `user_id`.
- `studio_graph(config)` supports **separate override namespaces** for the extractor model
  (`extractor_provider`, `extractor_model`, `extractor_temperature`, `extractor_api_key`,
  `extractor_base_url`, each falling back to the non-prefixed key) — good pattern to copy for any
  future independently-configurable dual-model setup.

**Enhancement tasks:**
- Switch to `MemoryConfiguration.from_runnable_config(config)` like the other three lessons, for
  consistent error behavior when `user_id` is missing.
- Add a test proving the two-model split works: stub `extractor_llm` and `llm` separately,
  assert only the extractor model's structured-output path is invoked by the update node.

### 5.3 `agent_with_fact_collection.py` — flexible multi-fact collection (vs. single profile)

- `Memory(BaseModel)`: single field `content: str`. Deliberately unstructured/flexible — the
  opposite design choice from `agent_with_structured_memory.py`'s fixed-field `UserProfile`.
- `_create_update_facts_node` builds `structured_agent = create_agent(model=llm, tools=[],
  response_format=ToolStrategy(schema=Memory, handle_errors=True))` **once**, at node-factory
  time (not per-invocation) — correct/efficient.
- **Storage model:** each fact gets `store.put(namespace, str(uuid.uuid4()), ...)` — a **new
  document per turn**, never overwritten, never deduplicated. Contrast directly with
  `agent_with_structured_memory.py`'s single fixed-key document. Facts can accumulate
  near-duplicates indefinitely — there is no consolidation step.
- `inspect_saved_facts(graph, user_id)` — demo-only helper reaching into `graph.store` directly
  (exposed on the compiled graph object) to enumerate everything under `("memories", user_id)`.

**Enhancement tasks:**
- **Unbounded growth is the real production gap here:** add either (a) a periodic
  consolidation/dedup node (LLM-driven merge of near-duplicate facts), or (b) a cap on stored
  facts per user with LRU or relevance-based eviction.
- Add a test asserting N distinct `store.put` calls after N distinct fact-bearing turns (proves
  no accidental overwrite), and a second test proving near-duplicate facts are NOT automatically
  merged today (documents the current limitation).

### 5.4 `agent_with_long_term_memory.py` — cross-thread bullet-list reflection

- Two-node graph: `call_model` (reads `store.get(("memory", user_id), "user_memory")`, injects as
  system prompt) → `write_memory` (regenerates the **entire** bullet-list summary from scratch
  each turn via `CREATE_MEMORY_INSTRUCTION`, then `store.put`s the whole thing back).
- **Key architectural note vs. `agent_with_fact_collection.py`:** this lesson **fully
  regenerates** memory text every turn (LLM shown old memory + new conversation, asked to produce
  a merged bullet list) rather than appending new documents. Token cost scales with total memory
  size on every single turn since the whole memory blob is both read and rewritten each time.
- `demo_conversation` uses one fixed `thread_id="thread-1"` + `user_id="leslie"` — **verified:
  the cross-thread claim in the docstring** ("knowledge survives across conversations") **is not
  actually demonstrated in `main()`'s demo**, which never uses a second thread_id. It's an
  architectural capability shown only by the state design (`user_id`-keyed store, separate from
  `thread_id`-keyed checkpointer), not exercised by the scripted demo.

**Enhancement tasks:**
- Add a second demo thread (`thread_id="thread-2"`, same `user_id="leslie"`) to
  `demo_conversation`/`main()` to actually demonstrate the cross-thread claim — currently
  unverified by the code itself.
- The full-regeneration-every-turn cost profile is the main optimization target: consider
  switching to `agent_with_fact_collection.py`'s append-only model, or a hybrid (append facts,
  periodically consolidate into the bullet summary) if both recency and cost matter.

### 5.5 `agent_with_multi_memory_coordination.py` — intelligent routing across 3 memory types

The most sophisticated lesson in this stage — coordinates **three** memory namespaces
(`("profile", user_id)`, `("todo", user_id)`, `("instructions", user_id)`) via a router node.

- `UpdateMemory(TypedDict): update_type: Literal["user", "todo", "instructions"]` bound as a
  **pseudo-tool** (`llm.bind_tools([UpdateMemory], parallel_tool_calls=False)`) — the model
  doesn't call a real function; it emits a structured "which memory type should I update"
  decision, which `route_message` (the conditional-edge function) reads out of
  `message.tool_calls[0]["args"]["update_type"]` to pick the next node. This "bind a TypedDict as
  a fake tool purely for routing" pattern is unique to this lesson in the repo.
- Graph topology: `task_orchestra` (router) → conditional edge → one of `{update_profile,
  update_todos, update_instructions}` → **back to `task_orchestra`** (loop, not `END`) — control
  returns to the router after any memory update, which returns `END` once the latest message has
  no more tool calls.
- `Profile` and `ToDo` Pydantic schemas both use `create_structured_agent` independently per
  updater node — a single "add a todo" turn costs at least 2 LLM calls (router decision + todo
  extraction), sometimes 3 including the final confirmation pass.
- `update_profile`/`update_todos` both call `store.put(namespace, str(uuid.uuid4()), ...)` —
  **same unbounded-accumulation gap as `agent_with_fact_collection.py`** (§5.3), applied here to
  profile and todo documents too.
- Each updater node returns a synthetic **tool message** (`{"role": "tool", "content": "...",
  "tool_call_id": ...}`) to satisfy the OpenAI tool-calling protocol's requirement that every
  `tool_calls` entry gets a matching tool response before the next assistant turn — removing this
  would break the conversation for OpenAI-compatible providers on the next turn.

**Enhancement tasks:**
- Same unbounded per-user document accumulation as §5.3 — apply the same consolidation/cap fix
  here for `profile`/`todo` namespaces. `ToDo.status` already models lifecycle (`"not
  started"|"in progress"|"done"|"archived"`) — a natural first fix is a periodic node that
  archives/prunes `"done"`/`"archived"` todos older than N days.
- Highest per-turn LLM-call-count lesson in the repo — if latency/cost is a concern, this is the
  first lesson to instrument with per-node latency logging.
- Add a test asserting `route_message` returns the correct next-node string for each of the three
  `update_type` literal values plus the `END` fallback when no tool call is present — this
  routing function is pure and trivially testable without any LLM.

### 5.6 Stage 05 production checklist

- Fix the `KeyError`-prone raw `config["configurable"]["user_id"]` access in
  `agent_with_structured_memory.py` to match its siblings' use of `MemoryConfiguration` (§5.2).
- Address unbounded per-user document growth in both `agent_with_fact_collection.py` (§5.3) and
  `agent_with_multi_memory_coordination.py`'s profile/todo namespaces (§5.5) — the single biggest
  structural gap in this stage for any lesson meant to run against real, long-lived users.
- Actually demonstrate the cross-thread memory claim in `agent_with_long_term_memory.py`'s demo
  (§5.4) — currently asserted only in the docstring.
- `route_message` in `agent_with_multi_memory_coordination.py` (§5.5) is the best pure-logic
  unit-test target in this stage.

